Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Locomotives Chew the Third Rail
- Story & Context: When a wildcat freight derailment rattled the ceiling of the old accelerator facility in early October, Otis Vance didn't stop singing—he plugged his bullet mic straight into an auxiliary beamline monitor while Vesna shredded through a weeping 7/8 scale. The crowd smelled ozone and scorched ballast stone as sparks dropped onto the concrete dance floor, turning panic into a stamping, four-hundred-person riot of heavy boots. Between the screech of locking steel wheels and the stuttering MPC kick, Vance roared the hook that christened the subterranean venue: "Train ran off the iron, but my copper still conducts!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Album art featuring a battered 2000s diesel locomotive grinding off glowing steel rails into a dark circular particle accelerator tunnel, shower of orange sparks illuminating cracked concrete, early digital video aesthetics, gritty Y2K cyber-industrial collage.

Track 2: Did the 4:15 Leave Without You?
- Story & Context: Dragan, an exiled brass player who spent his days hauling scrap at the railyard and his nights brooding near the defunct magnetic collimators, spilled half a thermos of plum rakija over his trumpet when the morning timetable board suddenly flickered back to life with ghost departures. The rhythm section seized his frantic, breathy improvisation, looping his stumbling triplets beneath a jagged Chicago slide groove that tasted like sour nicotine and copper pennies. He leaned over the lip of the ring trench, his voice cracked with three days of exhaust and longing, crying: "I sold my pocketwatch to buy five minutes of track!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A desolate underground platform illuminated by a malfunctioning split-flap departure timetable, amber warning lights casting silhouettes of an abandoned trumpet and a dripping violin bow on the greasy platform edge, foggy dystopian atmosphere.

Track 3: Otpad Ekspres
- Story & Context: Legend across the south docks says that on the winter night the municipal power authority cut the venue’s juice, Vesna chained her electrified fiddle directly to the accelerator's backup flywheel while the crowd dragged a derelict industrial handcar into the ring's service tunnel. The resulting acoustic boom rattled grease from the subterranean pipes as the brass section blew until their lips split, playing a furious twenty-minute dance that allegedly vibrated the freight tracks three miles above ground. As the cold morning air finally flooded down the intake vents, Otis shouted into the dead flywheel's hum: "Throw your coats upon the coal, the dead line’s rolling now!"
